# throne-backend.service — user unit that owns the throne-backend NestJS
# server: the long-running @nestjs/schedule cron host for keep-going,
# no-idling, the throne-work dispatch loop, the status.md writer, and the
# feature-flagged BullMQ worker. Mirrors throne-work.service (persistent
# daemon, boot-started, resurrectable) rather than throne-keep-going.service
# (timer-triggered oneshot), because a cron-hosting server is inherently a
# standing process.
#
# THRONE-OWNED TEMPLATE — not directly loadable by systemd. `{{THRONE_ROOT}}`
# (absolute path of the throne directory) and `{{NODE_BIN}}` (absolute path
# of the node interpreter, resolved through mise's `lts` symlink) are
# substituted at install time; only the rendered copy in the systemd user
# unit dir is a working unit. Install with:
#   cd <throne root> && ./bin/throne-cli install-services
#
# This template is added and installed no-clobber alongside the still-active
# throne-keep-going/throne-no-idling/throne-work units — it is NOT enabled
# by install-services and none of those old units are touched. Enabling
# throne-backend and retiring the old three units is a later, Regent-
# authorized operator decision.
#
# Dispatch delivers through the herdr pane-send primitive, so the server is
# useless without a reachable herdr session: Wants=/After= start throne-herdr
# first, mirroring throne-work.service's ordering. (herdr-server.service was
# retired — see RETIRED_LINUX_UNITS in src/install-services/linux.ts — so this
# orders after its consolidated-name successor throne-herdr.service instead.)
#
# BCL campaign (2026-08-14): throne-backend.service now also owns what used
# to be the separate throne-build.service's job, in-process
# (`SelfRebuildHostedWorker`, see src/throne-backend/self-rebuild.hosted-worker.ts) —
# it rebuilds this checkout when its source settles and restarts itself onto
# the new build, but ONLY once that build succeeds. Compile-success is not
# runtime-success, though: a generation can build cleanly and still crash or
# never reach READY=1. See ExecStartPre= below for the other half of that
# guarantee — the automatic rollback a second, separate builder process used
# to provide for free.

[Unit]
Description=throne-backend server — NestJS cron host for keep-going, no-idling, throne-work dispatch, status.md, and the BullMQ worker
Wants=throne-herdr.service
After=throne-herdr.service

StartLimitIntervalSec=120
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
# `Type=notify` gets `READY=1` start-up semantics for free (the process is
# considered started only once it reports readiness) and is required for
# `WatchdogSec=` below to mean anything.
#
# `NotifyAccess=all`, not the `main`-only default `Type=notify` would
# otherwise imply: this process sends its `sd_notify` datagrams through the
# `systemd-notify` CLI (see `src/throne-backend/sd-notify.ts`) because
# Node's `node:dgram` module has no Unix-domain datagram support to open
# that socket directly as the main process itself. `systemd-notify` runs as
# a *child* process of the main `node` process — same control group,
# different PID — and `NotifyAccess=main` matches by PID, so it would
# silently discard every notification this unit ever sends, exactly the
# "confident liar" failure this watchdog exists to avoid. `all` matches any
# process in the unit's control group, which covers that child.
NotifyAccess=all
Type=notify
# THE AUTOSCALER IS PERMANENTLY ARMED (the Lord's order of 2026-09-02). This
# env var is the alpha-autoscale worker's spawn switch
# (`src/alpha-autoscale/kill-switch.ts`); it defaults OFF in code and was set
# nowhere on any host, so the worker never spawned an Alpha anywhere until
# that day. Do not remove it to pause the court: the operator pause is
# `steering.autoscaleEnabled: false` in the live `config.user.ts` (the
# `/autoscaler off` skill), which the worker re-reads every tick without a
# restart. The launchd twin carries the same key in EnvironmentVariables.
Environment=THRONE_ALPHA_AUTOSCALE_ENABLED=1
WorkingDirectory={{THRONE_ROOT}}
# `{{NODE_BIN}}` is the mise `lts` symlink form, not a version-pinned path:
# mise repoints `lts` on upgrade, so a version-pinned path would silently
# break the unit again the next time node moves. Calling node directly
# (no login-shell wrapper) makes resolution independent of ambient PATH,
# which is unset before any login shell runs at boot.
# BCL campaign (2026-08-14): runs BEFORE every single start attempt --
# including every iteration of a Restart=always crash loop, not just the
# first. It decides, from a small durable marker (never from anything inside
# a dist.build.<gen> directory, so it survives a generation broken badly
# enough to be unimportable), whether the generation `dist` currently points
# at has already failed once without reaching READY=1. If so, it atomically
# repoints `dist` back to the last generation that DID reach READY=1 before
# ExecStart runs -- see scripts/throne-backend-rollback-guard.mjs's own
# header and src/throne-backend/generation-readiness-marker.ts for the full
# state machine and src/throne-backend/rollback-guard-real-systemd.spec.ts
# for the acceptance proof (a real systemd unit, a real broken generation,
# a real recovery). It NEVER exits non-zero itself -- an ExecStartPre
# failure would fail the whole start attempt, turning "couldn't decide" into
# "can never start again", which is strictly worse than doing nothing.
# ExecStartPre= (the rollback guard) is GONE. It repointed `dist` back to a
# known-good generation when a new one failed to reach READY=1 — a hazard that
# only exists when a reload publishes an immutable generation and restarts into
# it. The watcher compiles in place and relaunches the child, so there is no
# generation to roll back to and nothing to roll back from: the recovery is
# "fix the source", which the watcher already acts on.
# THE WATCH LOOP IS THE SERVICE (2026-08-26). This host is both the Lord's
# development environment and a 24/7 court, deliberately — he self-bootstraps,
# building the throne with the throne. systemd exists here so the watch loop
# runs unattended, not so a release artifact is supervised.
#
# The long-lived process is now the Nest CLI; the app is its CHILD. That is
# the whole fix. Previously ExecStart ran the app directly, so every reload
# had to go out through `systemctl restart` — a DELIBERATE stop, which
# `Restart=always` correctly declines to undo, and which `StartLimitBurst=`
# can refuse permanently. On 2026-08-26 a self-restart landed on a generation
# that never reached READY=1 and the court was down for FOUR HOURS, taking the
# Regent resurrection tick, the idle sweep, the autoscaler and the queue drain
# with it, because all fourteen workers are schedules inside this one process.
#
# Under the watcher a bad build crash-loops in the CHILD. systemd never sees a
# stop, the start limit is never touched, and fixing the source recompiles and
# relaunches automatically. The failure class is removed, not guarded.
#
# `nest start --watch` compiles to `dist` and spawns
# `<outDir>/<sourceRoot>/<entryFile>`, which has nowhere to carry the
# `throne-backend` subcommand argument — hence `src/throne-backend-main.ts`,
# an argument-free entry point that calls the same `runThroneBackendForever`.
# Invoked as the CLI's real JS entry rather than `npx nest` or the
# `node_modules/.bin` shim: a systemd user unit has a famously thin PATH, and
# a shim is a shell script that would need one. This is a plain node argv.
# --path is RELATIVE, deliberately. nest-cli resolves it against the workspace
# root and reports an ABSOLUTE path as "Could not find TypeScript
# configuration file" even when the file plainly exists — the same trap
# already recorded in scripts/build-and-publish-dist.mjs, and hit again here
# on 2026-08-26 during this very cutover. WorkingDirectory= above is the
# throne root, so a bare filename is both correct and unambiguous.
ExecStart={{NODE_BIN}} {{THRONE_ROOT}}/node_modules/@nestjs/cli/bin/nest.js start --watch --path tsconfig.build.json
# `Type=notify` start-up waits out `TimeoutStartSec=` before killing a unit
# that never sends `READY=1` (unset here inherits the manager default,
# ~90s). `READY=1` is now withheld until every long-lived worker with a
# genuine readiness condition reaches it (see
# `HostedWorkerRegistrarService.whenAllWorkersReady` in
# hosted-worker-registrar.service.ts), and that wait carries its own
# internal per-worker timeout (`DEFAULT_WORKER_READY_TIMEOUT_MS = 20_000`,
# same file) so a hung worker fails loudly and by name well before any
# systemd-level timeout would fire blind. `TimeoutStartSec=` here only has
# to clear that internal 20s ceiling with margin for process exit and
# journal flush, not guess at worker behavior itself: 45s leaves ~25s of
# margin above the 20s internal timeout, while still resolving far sooner
# than the ~90s inherited default would if left unset.
TimeoutStartSec=45
Restart=always
RestartSec=3
# The keep-going hosted worker (the only sender of `WATCHDOG=1`, see
# keep-going.hosted-worker.ts) ticks every 30 minutes
# (throne-keep-going.timer's own OnUnitActiveSec= cadence, mirrored
# in-process) and tolerates up to 2 consecutive failed ticks before
# withholding the ping on the 3rd (CONSECUTIVE_FAILURE_THRESHOLD = 3 in
# keep-going.hosted-worker.ts). That means two separate deadlines share this
# one number, and it has to clear both:
#   - One missed/delayed tick must survive: the gap between two successful
#     pings can stretch to ~2 cadences (3600s) before the next ping lands, so
#     WatchdogSec= must sit comfortably above 3600s or a single slow tick
#     kills the service outright (2400s's old bug).
#   - A genuinely persistent failure must still be caught well inside its
#     N x cadence = 3 x 1800s = 5400s theoretical worst case (pings continue
#     through the first 2 failures, so systemd only starts timing out after
#     the last of those), so WatchdogSec= must sit meaningfully below 5400s
#     rather than just being "some number bigger than 1800".
# 4200s (70 minutes) splits that gap: 600s (10 minutes) of margin above the
# one-missed-tick floor, 1200s (20 minutes) below the persistent-failure
# ceiling.
WatchdogSec=4200
SyslogIdentifier=throne-backend
# No ExecStart output redirection needed: systemd captures stdout/stderr to
# the journal by default (journalctl --user -u throne-backend.service).

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
